//! # ZCIR Classical Constant Integration
//!
//! Path: `src/classical/zcir/values/constant.rs`
//! Edition: Rust 2021
//! Minimum supported Rust version: 1.97
//! Safety: unsafe Rust is forbidden.
//!
//! ## Purpose
//!
//! Expose the canonical ZUIR constant representation through the ZCIR
//! classical-value API without defining a competing constant model.
//!
//! Classical computation requires portable representations of booleans,
//! integers, floating-point bit patterns, exact decimals, strings, bytes,
//! aggregates, symbolic values, and extension-defined values. The canonical
//! implementation of these representations already belongs to ZUIR.
//!
//! This module therefore provides:
//!
//! - Stable ZCIR import paths for canonical constant types.
//! - Explicit validation entry points for constants and constant values.
//! - Access to configurable validation budgets.
//! - A clear boundary between representation validation and semantic checks.
//!
//! ## Authority
//!
//! The following components retain their existing ownership:
//!
//! - `crate::zuir::values::constants` owns constant representations and
//!   local representation validation.
//! - `crate::zuir::types::type_def` owns canonical semantic type references.
//! - The frontend AST owns source literal syntax and source locations.
//! - The semantic model owns source-language meaning and type compatibility.
//! - The expression subsystem owns symbolic expressions and evaluation.
//! - ZUIR owns canonical IR-level value integration.
//! - Verification owns whole-program invariants.
//! - Lowering owns target-specific representations and feasibility checks.
//!
//! The relevant language contracts include:
//!
//! - `grammar/Zamani.g4`
//! - `grammar/specification/semantic-model.md`
//! - `grammar/specification/scalability-model.md`
//! - `grammar/specification/poco-reaf.md`
//! - `grammar/lexer/numeric-literals.g4`
//! - `src/zuir/ZUIR-SPEC.md`
//!
//! ## Ownership
//!
//! This file owns only the ZCIR-facing integration boundary.
//!
//! It does not define another `Constant`, `ConstantValue`, numeric wrapper,
//! aggregate representation, validation-error hierarchy, or budget type.
//!
//! It does not parse numeric source spellings, resolve names, infer types,
//! evaluate constants, allocate memory on behalf of a target, or lower
//! constants into machine-specific representations.
//!
//! ## POCO-REAF and scalability
//!
//! No universal limit on integer width, decimal precision, aggregate length,
//! string length, byte length, or number of constants is introduced here.
//!
//! Arbitrary-width values retain their canonical representations. No
//! conversion through native Rust numeric types is performed implicitly.
//!
//! The canonical validation API accepts explicit operational budgets.
//! An absent configured budget does not promise infinite memory, execution
//! time, address space, or physical resources.
//!
//! Exhausted budgets must be reported by the canonical validator rather
//! than interpreted as semantic invalidity or silently ignored.
//!
//! ## Validation boundary
//!
//! Successful local validation establishes only that the constant's
//! representation satisfies the canonical representation invariants.
//!
//! It does not establish:
//!
//! - That the declared type reference resolves.
//! - That the value is compatible with its declared type.
//! - That a symbolic expression can be evaluated.
//! - That an extension is registered or trusted.
//! - That an execution target supports the value.
//! - That a target has sufficient resources to realize it.
//!
//! Those checks belong to their respective authoritative subsystems.
//!
//! ## Integration contract
//!
//! `src/classical/zcir/values/mod.rs` declares this module and may re-export
//! the public items below.
//!
//! The ZCIR module root must register the completed values module only after
//! the corresponding source files and exports exist.
//!
//! `values/integer.rs`, `values/float.rs`, `values/aggregate.rs`, and
//! `values/symbolic.rs` must reuse these canonical types. They must not
//! introduce competing value enums or incompatible validation rules.
//!
//! Frontend semantic analysis supplies the canonical type reference.
//! ZCIR construction preserves the supplied type and value. The canonical
//! type verifier subsequently checks their semantic compatibility.
//!
//! ## Freeze criteria
//!
//! - Canonical value types are reused without duplication.
//! - Validation delegates to the authoritative ZUIR implementation.
//! - Explicit budgets are passed through unchanged.
//! - No host-width conversion or implicit numeric normalization occurs.
//! - No target-specific dependency or hardware assumption is introduced.
//! - The module compiles after its parent module is integrated.
//! - Unit and cross-module tests pass on the declared Rust baseline.

use crate::zuir::values::constants::{
    ArbitraryInteger,
    Constant,
    ConstantValidationBudget,
    ConstantValidationError,
    ConstantValue,
    ExactDecimal,
    ExtensionConstant,
    FloatBits,
};

// =============================================================================
// Canonical public types
// =============================================================================

/// Canonical typed constant.
///
/// The declared type is a canonical ZUIR `TypeRef`. Local constant
/// validation does not prove semantic type compatibility.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::Constant as ZcirConstant;

/// Canonical constant-value representation.
///
/// This is a re-export of the ZUIR value enum, not a separate ZCIR enum.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ConstantValue as ZcirConstantValue;

/// Canonical arbitrary-width integer representation.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ArbitraryInteger as ZcirInteger;

/// Canonical floating-point format and bit-pattern representation.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::FloatBits as ZcirFloatBits;

/// Canonical exact-decimal representation.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ExactDecimal as ZcirExactDecimal;

/// Canonical extension-defined constant representation.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ExtensionConstant as ZcirExtensionConstant;

/// Canonical operational validation budget.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ConstantValidationBudget
    as ZcirConstantValidationBudget;

/// Canonical representation-validation error.
pub use crate::zuir::values::constants::ConstantValidationError
    as ZcirConstantValidationError;

// =============================================================================
// Validation entry points
// =============================================================================

/// Validate a typed constant's canonical value representation.
///
/// This function delegates to ZUIR and introduces no additional validation
/// budget. It does not resolve or verify the constant's semantic type.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns the canonical ZUIR validation error if the value representation
/// is malformed or the validator encounters an implementation failure
/// represented by its error contract.
pub fn validate_constant(
    constant: &Constant,
) -> Result<(), ConstantValidationError> {
    constant.validate()
}

/// Validate a typed constant under an explicit operational budget.
///
/// The supplied budget is passed to the canonical implementation unchanged.
/// An exhausted budget is reported using the canonical error variant.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns a canonical representation-validation or budget-exhaustion
/// error.
pub fn validate_constant_with(
    constant: &Constant,
    budget: &ConstantValidationBudget,
) -> Result<(), ConstantValidationError> {
    constant.validate_with(budget)
}

/// Validate a constant value without imposing an additional configured
/// node-count or text-byte budget.
///
/// This validates the local representation only. It does not evaluate
/// symbolic values or verify compatibility with a semantic type.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns a canonical representation-validation error.
pub fn validate_value(
    value: &ConstantValue,
) -> Result<(), ConstantValidationError> {
    value.validate()
}

/// Validate a constant value using an explicit operational budget.
///
/// The canonical validator determines how nodes and encoded text contribute
/// to the budget. This adapter neither modifies nor overrides that policy.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns a canonical representation-validation or budget-exhaustion
/// error.
pub fn validate_value_with(
    value: &ConstantValue,
    budget: &ConstantValidationBudget,
) -> Result<(), ConstantValidationError> {
    value.validate_with(budget)
}

// =============================================================================
// Public API contract tests
// =============================================================================

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn zcir_constant_value_is_the_canonical_z uir_value_type() {
        // This test deliberately uses the canonical representation.
        // ZCIR must not introduce a second value enum.
        let canonical = ConstantValue::Boolean(true);
        let zcir: ZcirConstantValue = canonical.clone();

        assert_eq!(zcir, canonical);
        assert!(validate_value(&zcir).is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn accepts_arbitrary_width_integer_representation() {
        // No conversion to u64, u128, usize, or a native register width.
        let digits = "9".repeat(1_000);
        let integer = ArbitraryInteger::new(digits.clone())
            .expect("a canonical positive decimal integer should be valid");

        assert_eq!(integer.as_str(), digits);
        assert!(integer.validate().is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn validates_boolean_constant_value() {
        assert!(validate_value(&ConstantValue::Boolean(true)).is_ok());
        assert!(validate_value(&ConstantValue::Boolean(false)).is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn validates_empty_sequence() {
        let value = ConstantValue::Sequence(Vec::new());

        assert!(validate_value(&value).is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn preserves_ordered_sequence_elements() {
        let value = ConstantValue::Sequence(vec![
            ConstantValue::Boolean(true),
            ConstantValue::Boolean(false),
            ConstantValue::Boolean(true),
        ]);

        assert!(validate_value(&value).is_ok());

        match value {
            ConstantValue::Sequence(values) => {
                assert_eq!(values.len(), 3);
                assert_eq!(values[0], ConstantValue::Boolean(true));
                assert_eq!(values[1], ConstantValue::Boolean(false));
                assert_eq!(values[2], ConstantValue::Boolean(true));
            }
            _ => panic!("the canonical sequence representation was not preserved"),
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn accepts_explicit_unlimited_validation_budget() {
        let budget = ConstantValidationBudget::unlimited();
        let value = ConstantValue::Boolean(true);

        assert!(validate_value_with(&value, &budget).is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn reports_explicit_budget_exhaustion() {
        let budget = ConstantValidationBudget::new(Some(0), None);
        let value = ConstantValue::Boolean(true);

        assert_eq!(
            validate_value_with(&value, &budget),
            Err(ConstantValidationError::NodeBudgetExceeded),
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn explicit_text_budget_is_enforced_by_canonical_validator() {
        let budget = ConstantValidationBudget::new(None, Some(0));
        let value = ConstantValue::String("x".to_owned());

        assert_eq!(
            validate_value_with(&value, &budget),
            Err(ConstantValidationError::TextBudgetExceeded),
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn canonical_integer_constructor_rejects_noncanonical_encoding() {
        // The adapter must preserve canonical validation rather than
        // normalizing malformed input or accepting it silently.
        assert_eq!(
            ArbitraryInteger::new("01"),
            Err(ConstantValidationError::InvalidInteger),
        );

        assert_eq!(
            ArbitraryInteger::new("-0"),
            Err(ConstantValidationError::InvalidInteger),
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn preserves_float_format_and_bit_pattern() {
        let value = FloatBits::new("core::binary_format", "3ff")
            .expect("a nonempty format and valid hexadecimal bits");

        assert_eq!(value.format, "core::binary_format");
        assert_eq!(value.bits_hex, "3ff");
        assert!(value.validate().is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn validates_exact_decimal_without_native_float_conversion() {
        let coefficient = ArbitraryInteger::new("125")
            .expect("canonical coefficient");
        let exponent = ArbitraryInteger::new("-2")
            .expect("canonical exponent");

        let decimal = ExactDecimal::new(coefficient, exponent)
            .expect("valid exact decimal representation");

        assert_eq!(decimal.coefficient.as_str(), "125");
        assert_eq!(decimal.exponent.as_str(), "-2");
        assert!(decimal.validate().is_ok());
    }

    #[test]
    fn validates_extension_value_without_executing_it() {
        let extension = ExtensionConstant::new(
            "org.example.numeric",
            "extended_value",
            "1",
            "00ff",
        )
        .expect("valid opaque extension representation");

        assert_eq!(extension.namespace, "org.example.numeric");
        assert_eq!(extension.name, "extended_value");
        assert_eq!(extension.version, "1");
        assert_eq!(extension.payload_hex, "00ff");
    }

    #[test]
    fn repeated_validation_is_deterministic() {
        let value = ConstantValue::Sequence(vec![
            ConstantValue::Integer(
                ArbitraryInteger::new("123456789012345678901234567890")
                    .expect("valid arbitrary-width integer"),
            ),
            ConstantValue::String("portable".to_owned()),
        ]);

        assert_eq!(validate_value(&value), Ok(()));
        assert_eq!(validate_value(&value), Ok(()));
    }
}
